  "result": "At dawn, the city looks briefly undecided. Streetlights fade into the pale sky, buses begin their routes, and somewhere a baker slides the first tray of bread into an oven. These small actions rarely make the news, yet they hold the morning together.\n\nI like this hour because it belongs to nobody completely. Night has not quite released its claim, while day has not finished arriving. In that narrow interval, possibilities seem less demanding. A person can change direction without announcing it. A difficult thought can be set down beside a cup of tea and examined later. Even mistakes appear temporary, softened by blue light.\n\nPerhaps this is why hope often feels ordinary. It does not always arrive as a trumpet or a revelation. Sometimes it is simply the decision to wash a dish, answer a message, water a plant, or open the window again. The gesture is modest, but modesty is not the same as weakness. Roots are modest. So are foundations, hinges, and the quiet people who keep promises when nobody is watching.\n\nBy seven, the city has chosen a mood. Cars complain, phones glow, doors swing open, and the day gathers speed. I carry my small uncertainty into it, not expecting answers, only hoping to notice useful questions. There is freedom in that: we do not need to understand everything before taking the next honest step.\n\nOutside, sunlight touches a window above the bakery. For a moment, the glass shines like water. Someone inside laughs. World continues.",
